\title{PoseFixeR: Anomaly Detection and Correction for OpenPose Gait Analysis}
\description{
PoseFixeR cleans two-dimensional, video-based gait recordings produced by
OpenPose. It imports per-frame keypoint detections, flags the anatomical,
biomechanical, physical and low-confidence anomalies that arise when filming
in uncontrolled conditions, sets the offending keypoints aside, then imputes
and smooths the corrected trajectories so downstream gait analysis sees
continuous, physically plausible joint paths. It implements the method of
Sugiyama, Uno and Matsui (2023).
}
\section{Import and reshape}{

\itemize{
\item \code{\link[=import_json]{import_json()}} reads a batch of OpenPose per-frame JSON files into a list
of keypoint matrices (keypoint x \code{c("X", "Y", "P")}).
\item \code{\link[=reshaping]{reshaping()}} stacks those frames into \code{x}, \code{y} and confidence matrices
(frames x keypoints).
}
}

\section{Normalisation}{

\itemize{
\item \code{\link[=normalize]{normalize()}} expresses every keypoint relative to the neck and scales by
the hip-neck distance, removing camera position and distance.
}
}

\section{Keypoint features}{

\itemize{
\item \code{\link[=distance]{distance()}} Euclidean distance between two keypoints (a segment length).
\item \code{\link[=hdegree]{hdegree()}} hip joint angle and \code{\link[=kdegree]{kdegree()}} knee joint angle, each measured
from three keypoints.
}
}

\section{Anomaly detection and correction}{

\itemize{
\item \code{\link[=anomaly]{anomaly()}} flags statistical outliers in a numeric series.
\item \code{\link[=posefixer]{posefixer()}} is the main entry point: it runs the full
detect-correct-impute-smooth workflow on the reshaped trajectories.
}
